text:
anika: quick question — i'm about to land a tiny terraform change that adds a `redwood.org/config-checksum` annotation on model Deployments (sha256 of the per-model config + feature flags blob). goal is to make k8s trigger a rolling update when the mounted ConfigMap changes. any blockers? :eyes:

mike-l: what's the plan for noisy flag flips? if teams toggle flags often we could end up restarting pods too much. are you deduping or rate-limiting annotation bumps?

anika: good call. plan is to compute the checksum from the canonical config obj in TF (we pull the file contents + flags json, sha256). we only apply the new annotation when the checksum actually changes. also going to add a small cooldown in the CI pipeline so multiple quick commits collapse into one apply. example TF output block (WIP):

```
# terraform pseudo
locals {
  config_blob = file("./configs/model-x.json")
  cfg_hash    = sha256(local.config_blob)
}
resource "kubernetes_deployment" "model_x" {
  metadata {
    annotations = {
      "redwood.org/config-checksum" = local.cfg_hash
    }
  }
  # ...
}
```

anika: fwiw i considered using the ConfigMap checksum annotation on the CM itself but hits where CM updates don't always cascade to pods depending on how it's mounted, so deploying the checksum on the Deployment is more reliable for our rollout semantics.

sarah-k: how will this interact with kv-cache and runtime in-memory flags? do we do anything special at runtime to avoid warm-cache churn?

anika: runtime team added an admin endpoint to invalidate model-specific kv-cache (POST /admin/models/:id/invalidate-cache). the rollout will still restart pods, but if you want zero-restart flag flips we can have the TF apply trigger a webhook to the runtime admin endpoint instead of bumping annotation. i'm thinking default = annotation (safe) + an opt-in 'no-restart' toggle that calls the webhook.

luis: small nit — prefer using the Deployment annotation rather than mutating labels; annotations are less likely to be used by selectors and won't affect svc selectors etc. also let's document behaviour in ops-runbook.

devops-bot: terraform plan for branch/feature/config-checksum completed: 0 errors, 2 changes (1 update, 1 replace).

mike-l: can we do a canary? e.g. patch the deployment strategy to only replace 10% of replicas first for 10m, then continue. safer for models under live traffic.

tim-e: +1 on canary. i'd prefer we use our rollout controller to create a canary by temporarily scaling a separate replicaSet/Deployment. easier to measure latency regressions. @eng-runtime please weigh in.

anika: yea i'll open a PR with the TF diff + a short ops note (how to revert, how to call the webhook if you opt out of restarts). for now i'll hold the plan and sync with @eng-runtime on the canary strategy. thanks all :thumbsup:
